Subject: [PATCH] vio: generate the automatic certificate's key on P-256

When no certificate is configured the server generates a self-signed one
at startup, with a 4096-bit RSA key. Finding two 2048-bit primes is a
random search, and with wolfSSL's portable arithmetic in wasm each
Miller-Rabin round is a 2048-bit exponentiation over 72 limbs, so
startup took anywhere from under a second to fifteen.

Generate a P-256 key instead. TLS 1.3 requires compliant implementations
to support ECDSA with P-256 and SHA-256, and ECDSA/P-256 is widely
supported by TLS 1.2 implementations. MariaDB's own client negotiates
TLS 1.3 against it. wolfSSL is built with ECC, the certificate continues
to use SHA-256, and key generation requires only a scalar
multiplication: startup takes 0.05 to 0.09 s.

P-256 provides roughly the same 128-bit security strength as 3072-bit
RSA, for a self-signed certificate that lives only as long as the server
process.

diff --git a/vio/viosslfactories.c b/vio/viosslfactories.c
--- a/vio/viosslfactories.c
+++ b/vio/viosslfactories.c
@@ -105,13 +105,13 @@
   EVP_PKEY_CTX *ctx;
   EVP_PKEY *pkey = NULL;
 
-  if (!(ctx= EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_id(EVP_PKEY_RSA, NULL)))
+  if (!(ctx= EVP_PKEY_CTX_new_id(EVP_PKEY_EC, NULL)))
     return NULL;
 
   if (EVP_PKEY_keygen_init(ctx) <= 0)
     goto end;
 
-  if (EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_rsa_keygen_bits(ctx, 4096) <= 0)
+  if (EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_ec_paramgen_curve_nid(ctx, NID_X9_62_prime256v1) <= 0)
     goto end;
 
   if (EVP_PKEY_keygen(ctx, &pkey) <= 0)
